  Real cluster vs synthetic test values — why numbers differ
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  The path_hybrid_how_it_work_search doc uses synthetic values:
    α_intra=5µs, α_cross=80µs, T_block=1ms

  The real cluster produced:
    α_intra=111µs, α_cross=122µs, T_block=2.608ms

  Key differences and their impact on the winning plan:

  1. T_block is 2.6× larger (real hardware is slower for this tiny model)
     → T_bubble absolute cost is larger (7.8ms vs 3ms in synthetic)
     → But the WINNER is the same: pp=4 tp=2 dp=1

  2. α_intra ≈ α_cross on this cluster (bond-local Ethernet is fast)
     → TP and PP latencies are closer together than in synthetic case
     → Still, BW_intra (27.5 GB/s) >> BW_cross (2.7 GB/s) dominates for large tensors

  3. PP cost is smaller in reality (0.6ms vs 21ms synthetic)
     → Small activation tensors (64KB) make PP latency-dominated, not BW-dominated
     → β_cross × 64KB = 0.37ns × 65536 = 0.024ms — tiny compared to α_cross

  4. The decision logic is the same:
     Eliminating DP AllReduce (cross-node gradient sync) is always worth it
     when dp=1 is achievable (pp×tp = world_size).

